# Durations: functions about a `duration` (R-62, R-66; docs/grammar.md
# "Functions"). A duration is months, days and an exact part; it is
# written `1y 1mo 1w 1d 1h 1m 1s 1ms 1us 1ns`, integers with one unit each
# and largest first (`1h30m`), or in ISO 8601 (`"P1M"`, `"PT6H"`).
package duration

#| A duration from its friendly (`1h30m`) or ISO 8601 (`P1M`, `PT6H`) text; no value for any other text. `duration(s)` is the same, checked at compile time for a literal.
#| example: let ttl = duration.parse(cfg.ttl)
fn parse(text: string) -> duration?

#| The duration as a whole number of `unit` ("nanoseconds" .. "weeks", a day as 24 hours; "months", "years" for a duration of whole months); no value when it is not a whole number of them.
#| example: let hours = duration.total(ttl, "hours")
fn total(d: duration, unit: string) -> int?
